---------------------------------------------------------------------

Why this file is here, and why these terms rather than the AGPL that
governs the rest of the repository.

The patches in this directory are two kinds of content. The context
lines — every line a patch quotes so that it can be applied — are
Google's code, and they stay under Chromium's own BSD-3-Clause licence
whatever anyone else decides.

The lines Fury adds, including the files under components/fury/ that
the series creates outright, are BSD-3-Clause by choice. BSD-3-Clause
imposes nothing on the licence of surrounding or added work, so these
could have been AGPL alongside the rest of the project. They are not,
so that a Chromium built from this series carries one consistent set
of terms, and no copyleft obligation reaches somebody who only wanted
a browser.

This split was decided in docs/10-legal-licensing.md, which says to
keep the patches in a separate directory with an explicit LICENSE
inside it. This is that file.

The source files the series adds carry Chromium's own three-line
header, referring to "the LICENSE file". Read inside the Chromium
checkout where that code compiles, it resolves to core/src/LICENSE —
Chromium's BSD-3-Clause text. Read here, in a patch, it means this
one. The terms are the same either way, which is the point.
